---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import ClassRef from '../../components/ClassRef.astro';
import Demo from '../../components/Demo.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import ApiTable from '../../components/ApiTable.astro';
import DsaScore from '../../components/DsaScore.astro';
import api from '@busy-office/ui/api';
import { createRequire } from 'node:module';
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

/* The size argument below is computed from the shipped artifact — the
   hand-typed "10.3%" had drifted to a real 6.0% before anyone noticed
   (2026-08-21 sweep). Same projection, live numbers.

   The DENOMINATOR is derived from the same read as the numerator, and that
   is the whole point of this block. It was a hand-typed `12` while this
   stylesheet grew to 26 glyphs (137.1's toolbar set on 2026-08-24, then
   row-edit's two on 2026-08-27), so the projection divided 26 glyphs' worth
   of bytes by 12 and published a 200-icon set at 148 kB against a true 68.
   One read, so the two cannot drift apart again.

   That fix made the denominator LIVE and left it UNASSERTED, which is the
   same shape one step on: `iconCss.match(...) ?? []` yields a Set of size 0
   when the glyph selectors are ever renamed, and `iconBytes / 0` publishes
   "Infinity kB" to this page with nothing failing the build. Measured, not
   reasoned — the regex was run against a stylesheet carrying no modifiers.
   So the count is RECONCILED below against `api.json`, which extract-api.mjs
   derives from the SOURCE css while this regex reads the SHIPPED MIN css:
   two independent derivations of one fact, which catches a partial parse as
   well as an empty one. Deliberately no hand-typed floor — a literal minimum
   would be the very decaying constant this block exists to have removed. */
const require = createRequire(import.meta.url);
const iconCss = readFileSync(require.resolve('@busy-office/ui/css/components/icon.min'), 'utf8');
const iconBytes = iconCss.length;
const totalBytes = readFileSync(require.resolve('@busy-office/ui/css/min'), 'utf8').length;
const iconShare = ((iconBytes / totalBytes) * 100).toFixed(1);
const glyphCount = new Set(iconCss.match(/\.bo-icon--[a-z0-9-]+/g) ?? []).size;
const apiGlyphCount = api.components.icon.variants.length;
if (glyphCount !== apiGlyphCount)
  throw new Error(
    `icon: parsed ${glyphCount} glyph modifiers from the shipped min CSS, but api.json declares ${apiGlyphCount}. ` +
      'The size projection below DIVIDES by this count, so a bad parse ships a wrong or Infinite kB figure ' +
      'rather than failing — and that figure is the published arithmetic behind roadmap 40.1.',
  );
const totalKb = Math.round(totalBytes / 1024);
const catalogueKb = Math.round((iconBytes / glyphCount) * 200 / 1024);

/* The showcase eight are an EDITORIAL list, not a mirror — they are the glyphs
   judged to have earned a place, which no artifact records. `check:markup`
   already reconciles each rendered `bo-icon--*` class against api.json, so a
   renamed or deleted glyph fails the build here (red-proved 2026-08-31, Slice
   229: renaming `.bo-icon--doc` in the source CSS left core green and api.json
   at 26 variants — 227.3's count guard cannot see a rename — and `check-markup`
   exited 1 with 116 problems). */
const glyphs = ['doc', 'invoice', 'cart', 'check-circle', 'truck', 'box', 'chart', 'grid'];

/* DEPRECATED (roadmap 53.2). Kept for the deprecation window (next major),
   shown separately so the showcase stops implying they earned their place the
   way the eight above did.

   This block used to justify the deprecation with "zero pattern screens in
   these docs render these — only this page's own showcase did". That census
   was exact on 2026-08-19 and false when read on 2026-09-06 (roadmap 292.1):
   /patterns/app-frame renders --building, three other docs pages render
   --settings, and the reference suite gives two of its six modules --user and
   --settings as their identity. A deprecation does not stop existing renders,
   so it can never rest on a count of them; icon.css's header carries the same
   correction beside its NO FIGURES rule.

   DERIVED, not hand-typed — the one mirror on this page that `check:markup`
   structurally cannot see, because a wrongly-listed glyph is still a class the
   framework ships (Slice 229). It was `['settings','barcode','building','user']`
   against four `/* DEPRECATED` blocks in the same stylesheet, so deprecating a
   fifth glyph would have left this page showing it among the eight that earned
   their place, under a badge still reading 4, with nothing failing.

   Read from the UNMINIFIED shipped css, since minification drops the comments
   that carry the deprecation. Reconciled against a second, independent count of
   the markers themselves, so a partial parse is caught as well as an empty one —
   and deliberately no hand-typed floor, which would be the decaying constant
   227.1 removed from this same block.

   The trade, named rather than discovered: the marker count is a raw text
   count, so prose in `icon.css` that merely NAMES the word DEPRECATED breaks
   this build. That is the assertion-trips-on-its-own-explanation shape CLAUDE.md
   warns about — but it fails LOUDLY with the counts in the message, which is the
   safe direction of that trap, and the alternative (matching only the structural
   form) is what would silently under-report. Accepted deliberately. */
const iconSrcCss = readFileSync(require.resolve('@busy-office/ui/css/components/icon'), 'utf8');
const deprecatedGlyphs = [...iconSrcCss.matchAll(/\/\*[^*]*\*+(?:[^/*][^*]*\*+)*\/\s*\.bo-icon--([a-z0-9-]+)/g)]
  .filter((m) => m[0].includes('DEPRECATED'))
  .map((m) => m[1]);
const deprecationMarkers = (iconSrcCss.match(/DEPRECATED/g) ?? []).length;
if (deprecatedGlyphs.length !== deprecationMarkers)
  throw new Error(
    `icon: parsed ${deprecatedGlyphs.length} deprecated glyph(s) from the shipped css, but it carries ` +
      `${deprecationMarkers} DEPRECATED marker(s). This page publishes that set as the answer to "which ` +
      'glyphs should I stop using", so a partial parse must fail rather than under-report.',
  );
const undeclared = deprecatedGlyphs.filter((g) => !api.components.icon.variants.includes(`bo-icon--${g}`));
if (undeclared.length) throw new Error(`icon: deprecated glyph(s) not shipped: ${undeclared.join(', ')}`);
const bothLists = glyphs.filter((g) => deprecatedGlyphs.includes(g));
if (bothLists.length)
  throw new Error(
    `icon: glyph(s) in BOTH the showcase and the deprecated list: ${bothLists.join(', ')}. ` +
      'Deprecating a showcased glyph means removing it from the editorial eight above.',
  );

const markup = `<!-- Decorative (label carries the meaning). bo-sidebar-nav__icon is the rail's
     slot — it sizes the box and the collapsed rail is derived from it: -->
<a class="bo-sidebar-nav__link" href="/invoices">
  <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--invoice bo-sidebar-nav__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span class="bo-sidebar-nav__label">Invoices</span>
</a>

<!-- Icon-only control labels ITSELF, never the icon: -->
<button class="bo-btn bo-btn--ghost bo-btn--icon" aria-label="Close">
  <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--close" aria-hidden="true"></span>
</button>

<!-- Your own glyph in one line: point --bo-icon-src at any single-color SVG. -->
<style>
  .my-icon--rocket { --bo-icon-src: url("data:image/svg+xml,<svg …></svg>"); }
</style>
<span class="bo-icon my-icon--rocket" aria-hidden="true"></span>`;
const customIcon = `<span class="bo-icon" aria-hidden="true"
  style="--bo-icon-src: url('data:image/svg+xml,<svg xmlns=&quot;http://www.w3.org/2000/svg&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 24 24&quot; fill=&quot;none&quot; stroke=&quot;%23000&quot; stroke-width=&quot;2&quot; stroke-linecap=&quot;round&quot;><path d=&quot;M12 5v14M5 12h14&quot;/></svg>')"></span>
<span>Add line</span>`;

/* `bothLists` above compares two LISTS — the editorial eight against the derived
   deprecated four — and roadmap 292.4 is what that structurally cannot see:
   `--settings` was deprecated in the css and simultaneously taught as canonical
   in the two places a reader copies from, the `markup` block above and the "In
   context" demo. Both are hand-written literals belonging to neither list, so
   the guard was blind to the exact contradiction it exists to catch.

   So assert the property where the reader actually gets the class: every
   `bo-icon--*` a human typed on this page — in the copyable block or in a live
   demo — must not be one this page's own Deprecated section tells them to stop
   using. The two GENERATED showcases are exempt for free rather than by an
   exception: they build the class through an interpolation, which a literal scan
   cannot match, and the deprecated showcase must keep rendering the four to show
   what they look like.

   Scanned from this file's own source, TEMPLATE REGION ONLY. Frontmatter is
   deliberately excluded because this comment and the code under it name glyph
   classes, and an assertion that trips on its own explanation is a detector that
   cannot pass (CLAUDE.md). That is not hypothetical here — the editorial-eight
   comment above names a glyph in prose, and scanning the whole file would flag it.

   RECONCILED, because a regex that silently stopped matching would report a clean
   page rather than fail: each source's parsed count plus its interpolated
   occurrences must equal a raw count of the bare prefix, and every parsed name
   must be a variant `api.json` declares. Count the raw thing; do not trust the
   parse.

   NAMED PATH, not `import.meta.url` — measured, and the reconciliation above is
   what caught it on the first run. At build time this frontmatter executes as
   `apps/docs/dist/pages/components/icon.astro.mjs`, so `import.meta.url` reads
   the COMPILED module: the first attempt parsed 15 hand-written + 4 interpolated
   against 24 bare occurrences and failed loudly rather than scanning the wrong
   artifact in silence. The repo's existing `../../../../../` idiom works from
   either location only because `src/` and `dist/` sit at equal depth, which is
   a coincidence worth naming. A rename of this file breaks the read with ENOENT,
   which is the safe direction. */
const selfSrc = readFileSync(
  new URL('../../../../../apps/docs/src/pages/components/icon.astro', import.meta.url),
  'utf8',
);
const template = selfSrc.slice(selfSrc.indexOf('\n---\n', 3) + 5);
const taught = [];
for (const [label, text] of [['the markup block', markup], ['a live demo', template]]) {
  const named = [...text.matchAll(/bo-icon--([a-z0-9-]+)/g)].map((m) => m[1]);
  const interpolated = (text.match(/bo-icon--\$\{/g) ?? []).length;
  const bare = (text.match(/bo-icon--/g) ?? []).length;
  if (named.length + interpolated !== bare)
    throw new Error(
      `icon: parsed ${named.length} hand-written glyph(s) + ${interpolated} interpolated from ${label}, ` +
        `but it carries ${bare} occurrence(s) of the class prefix. This guard publishes "no deprecated ` +
        'glyph is taught here", so a partial parse must fail rather than under-report.',
    );
  const unshipped = named.filter((g) => !api.components.icon.variants.includes(`bo-icon--${g}`));
  if (unshipped.length)
    throw new Error(`icon: ${label} names glyph(s) api.json does not declare: ${unshipped.join(', ')}`);
  taught.push(...named.map((g) => [label, g]));
}
const taughtDeprecated = taught.filter(([, g]) => deprecatedGlyphs.includes(g));
if (taughtDeprecated.length)
  throw new Error(
    'icon: deprecated glyph(s) taught as canonical on this page: ' +
      `${taughtDeprecated.map(([label, g]) => `${g} (in ${label})`).join(', ')}. ` +
      'The Deprecated section tells readers to stop using these, so the copyable block and the live ' +
      'demos must not hand them the same class. Point --bo-icon-src at your own SVG instead, or use a ' +
      'glyph this page still recommends.',
  );

/* Roadmap 292.5, both clauses. The guard above asks whether a class taught here
   is DEPRECATED; these two ask whether it is the same class, and the same
   mechanism, the page itself demonstrates one section higher.

   Same structural cause in both: six of this page's seven demo sections
   hand-write their preview and then `markup` above rewrites it, which is the
   recipe's "never write the preview and code twice". The one section using
   <Demo code={…} /> is the only one that cannot drift, and it was the only one
   showing --bo-icon-src. Converting the rest is not the fix taken here — the
   in-context demo needs a three-item rail and an aria-current the copyable
   sample must not carry, and routing it through an interpolated generator would
   blind the 292.4 guard above, which matches LITERAL classes on purpose. So the
   two artefacts stay separate and the property between them is asserted. */

/* Clause 1: the sidebar-link sample a reader copies must carry the same slot
   classes as the live demo beside it. The glyph modifier is excluded — which
   glyph each shows is editorial, and comparing it would fire on a correct page.
   What is NOT editorial is bo-sidebar-nav__icon: sidebar-nav.css sizes that slot
   at 1.3em and derives the collapsed rail's 3.25rem from it, so a sample without
   it mis-aligns every label a reader builds. The copyable block omitted it while
   the demo one section above carried it, which is the defect this catches. */
const slotClasses = (src, label) => {
  const links = [...src.matchAll(/<a[^>]*\bclass="[^"]*\bbo-sidebar-nav__link\b[^"]*"[^>]*>([\s\S]*?)<\/a>/g)];
  if (!links.length) throw new Error(`icon: ${label} carries no .bo-sidebar-nav__link sample to compare.`);
  const sets = links.map((m) => {
    const spans = [...m[1].matchAll(/<span[^>]*\bclass="([^"]*\bbo-icon\b[^"]*)"/g)].map((s) => s[1]);
    if (spans.length !== 1)
      throw new Error(
        `icon: a .bo-sidebar-nav__link in ${label} holds ${spans.length} icon span(s), expected exactly 1. ` +
          'This guard publishes "the copyable sample matches the demo", so an ambiguous parse must fail.',
      );
    return spans[0].trim().split(/\s+/).filter((c) => !c.startsWith('bo-icon--')).sort().join(' ');
  });
  const distinct = [...new Set(sets)];
  if (distinct.length !== 1)
    throw new Error(`icon: ${label}'s ${links.length} sidebar links disagree on slot classes: ${distinct.join(' | ')}`);
  return { classes: distinct[0], count: links.length };
};
const copiedSlot = slotClasses(markup, 'the copyable markup block');
const liveSlot = slotClasses(template, 'the in-context demo');
if (copiedSlot.classes !== liveSlot.classes)
  throw new Error(
    `icon: the sidebar-link sample a reader copies renders different classes than the live demo beside it — ` +
      `copyable block [${copiedSlot.classes}] vs ${liveSlot.count} live link(s) [${liveSlot.classes}]. ` +
      'A reader pastes the block and gets a rail that does not look like the one on this page.',
  );

/* Clause 2: the custom-glyph line the copyable block teaches must be the property
   icon.css itself calls THE mechanism, not `mask-image`. DERIVED from the shipped
   stylesheet rather than hard-coded, so renaming the property fails here instead
   of leaving the page teaching a name nothing reads.

   The two are not interchangeable, and this is measured rather than argued
   (browser, built page, 2026-09-06): a consumer rule setting mask-image paints
   when it is unlayered and computes to `none` from inside @layer bo-primitives,
   because .bo-icon's own layered declaration then wins and resolves to nothing.
   The custom property paints from either position — it has no competitor.

   The Set is the reconciliation: the shipped css carries the declaration twice
   (autoprefixed -webkit- and plain), so a count is not the assertion — one
   DISTINCT property name is. Zero names, or two, fails rather than guessing.

   ASSERTED ON THE DECLARATION, NOT ON A SUBSTRING, and the red-proof is what
   forced that. The first version asked whether `markup` merely CONTAINED the
   property name; swapping the taught line to `background-image:` left the guard
   GREEN, because the stanza's own explanatory comment names --bo-icon-src one
   line above the rule. That is CLAUDE.md's assert-on-structure rule arriving
   from its other side: prose beside a change is supposed to name the thing, so a
   raw-text check can be SATISFIED by an explanation exactly as easily as it can
   be tripped by one. Comments are stripped first, and the property must appear
   as a declaration. */
const markupCode = markup.replace(/<!--[\s\S]*?-->/g, '');
const maskProps = [...new Set([...iconSrcCss.matchAll(/mask-image:\s*var\((--[a-z0-9-]+)\)/g)].map((m) => m[1]))];
if (maskProps.length !== 1)
  throw new Error(
    `icon: parsed ${maskProps.length} distinct custom propert(ies) behind mask-image in the shipped css ` +
      `(${maskProps.join(', ') || 'none'}), expected exactly 1. The copyable block below is checked against ` +
      'that name, so an unresolved parse must fail rather than skip the check.',
  );
if (!new RegExp(`${maskProps[0]}\\s*:`).test(markupCode))
  throw new Error(
    `icon: the copyable markup block never DECLARES ${maskProps[0]}, which icon.css calls THE mechanism ` +
      '(comments stripped before this check — naming it in prose is not teaching it). ' +
      'Teaching a different custom-glyph route here contradicts the section above it.',
  );
if (/mask-image\s*:/.test(markupCode))
  throw new Error(
    'icon: the copyable markup block sets mask-image directly. That override only paints where the ' +
      `consumer rule outranks @layer bo-components; teach ${maskProps[0]}, which does not depend on layering.`,
  );
---
<Gallery title="Icon" description="CSS-rendered icons: an inline SVG mask painted by currentColor, sized in em so they track the surrounding font size and density. No JS, no font files.">
  <p class="demo-note">
    CSS-rendered icons: an inline-SVG <code>mask-image</code> painted by
    <code>currentColor</code> — real, <strong>themable</strong> glyphs with zero
    JS, zero font files, no network requests. Sized <code>1em</code>, so they track
    the surrounding font size (and density) automatically. The set is deliberately
    small — enough ERP glyphs to prove the mechanism, never a catalogue; adding
    your own is one line of CSS — or paste inline SVG with
    <code>fill="currentColor"</code>, which is an equally first-class answer.
    <strong>Not as the only label for a meaning the reader has to
    learn</strong> — an icon-only control works when the glyph is already a
    convention (close, search, settings) and space is genuinely tight, and
    then it still needs an <code>aria-label</code>, since these glyphs are
    <code>aria-hidden</code> decoration. It fails the moment the meaning is
    domain-specific: nobody recognises a mark for "post goods issue", so
    that button needs its words. And where no glyph in any set reads as the
    thing — "accounts payable" has none — use letters: an initials
    <a href={base + '/components/badge'}>badge</a> is honest where an
    approximate icon is confidently wrong.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The set</h2>
    <div class="bo-cluster" style="--bo-cluster-gap: var(--bo-space-4); font-size: 1.5rem">
      {glyphs.map((g) => (
        <span style="display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: var(--bo-space-1)">
          <span class={`bo-icon bo-icon--${g}`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <code style="font-size: var(--bo-font-size-xs)">--{g}</code>
        </span>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Convenience presets for ERP concepts common enough
    to be worth shipping a name for — each is just a value of
    <code>--bo-icon-src</code>, same as your own would be (next section).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Deprecated <span class="bo-badge bo-badge--warning">{deprecatedGlyphs.length}</span></h2>
    <div class="bo-cluster bo-u-text-muted" style="--bo-cluster-gap: var(--bo-space-4); font-size: 1.5rem">
      {deprecatedGlyphs.map((g) => (
        <span style="display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: var(--bo-space-1)">
          <span class={`bo-icon bo-icon--${g}`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <code style="font-size: var(--bo-font-size-xs)">--{g}</code>
        </span>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Kept shipping (removing published classes is a
    next-major change, not a tidy-up) but no longer recommended: each was only
    ever one example value of <code>--bo-icon-src</code>, so point that at your
    own SVG instead (next section). Screens that already render one keep
    working until the next major — deprecating a glyph deprecates the
    <em>recommendation</em>, not the class.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Themable — it's just <code>currentColor</code></h2>
    <p style="font-size: 1.5rem">
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--check-circle" aria-hidden="true" style="color: var(--bo-color-success-text)"></span>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--truck" aria-hidden="true" style="color: var(--bo-color-accent)"></span>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--box" aria-hidden="true" style="color: var(--bo-color-danger-text)"></span>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--chart" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Set <code>color</code> (or inherit it) and the glyph
    follows — semantic tokens, dark mode, and brand presets all work with no
    icon-specific wiring. Under forced-colors the icons repaint in the system text
    color instead of vanishing (mask icons need an explicit opt-out; shipped).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Sizes — font-size IS the size</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--invoice" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--invoice" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size: 1.5rem"></span>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--invoice" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size: 2rem"></span>
      <span class="bo-icon bo-icon--invoice" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size: 3rem"></span>
    </p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">No size modifiers: <code>1em</code> means an icon in
    a compact table row is smaller than one in a spacious header for free.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>In context — sidebar links and icon buttons</h2>
    <nav class="bo-sidebar-nav demo-frame" aria-label="Icon demo" style="max-inline-size: 14rem">
      <ul>
        <li><a class="bo-sidebar-nav__link" href="#" aria-current="page"><span class="bo-icon bo-icon--invoice bo-sidebar-nav__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="bo-sidebar-nav__label">Invoices</span></a></li>
        <li><a class="bo-sidebar-nav__link" href="#"><span class="bo-icon bo-icon--check-circle bo-sidebar-nav__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="bo-sidebar-nav__label">Approvals</span></a></li>
        <li><a class="bo-sidebar-nav__link" href="#"><span class="bo-icon bo-icon--truck bo-sidebar-nav__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="bo-sidebar-nav__label">Shipments</span></a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <p style="margin-block-start: var(--bo-space-3)">
      <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--ghost bo-btn--icon" aria-label="Close"><span class="bo-icon bo-icon--close" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
      <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--ghost bo-btn--icon" aria-label="Reports"><span class="bo-icon bo-icon--chart" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
    </p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Composes with the existing
    <code>.bo-sidebar-nav__icon</code> slot and <code>.bo-btn--icon</code> —
    decorative icons carry <code>aria-hidden</code>; an icon-only button labels
    <em>itself</em> with <code>aria-label</code>, never the icon.
    Both buttons here pass the opener's test deliberately: a cross for
    close and a bar chart for reports are conventions a reader already
    holds. The same treatment on a domain action — "post goods issue" —
    would not be, and that button gets its words. Neither glyph here is one
    of the deprecated ones above — copy what this page still recommends.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Any icon you like — one custom property</h2>
    <p>The glyphs above are not a privileged list: each one is just a value of
    <code>--bo-icon-src</code>. Point that property at any SVG and the element gets the
    framework's sizing, <code>currentColor</code> painting, density tracking and
    forced-colors handling with <strong>no new class from us</strong>.</p>
    <Demo code={customIcon} />
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">That works with <a href="https://lucide.dev">Lucide</a>,
    <a href="https://heroicons.com">Heroicons</a> or your own brand mark — copy the SVG,
    percent-encode <code>#</code> as <code>%23</code>, and set the property. Icons drawn
    with <code>stroke="currentColor"</code> need no edit at all, because the mask ignores
    the SVG's own colours and paints with yours.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      <strong>Why there is no 200-icon catalogue here</strong>, since that is the obvious
      thing to ask for: the <strong>{glyphCount}</strong> glyphs shipped today are
      <strong>{iconShare}% of the whole framework</strong> by size, because every glyph is
      an inlined SVG. At that rate two hundred would add roughly
      <strong>{catalogueKb}&nbsp;kB</strong>, against a whole framework that is
      <strong>{totalKb}&nbsp;kB</strong> — to compete with icon sets that are better
      maintained and free. One property is the smaller answer and the more general one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Your own artwork — which mechanism, and when none</h2>
    <p>Four questions come up as "can we have SVG for this?". Three already have an
    answer that ships, and the fourth is a no.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">You want</th><th scope="col">Use</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">A one-colour glyph</th>
            <td><code>--bo-icon-src</code> on <code>.bo-icon</code></td>
            <td>It is a <strong>mask</strong>, so it takes your text colour and follows the
            theme, density and forced-colours handling automatically</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">A multi-colour mark — a logo, a vendor identicon</th>
            <td>a plain <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>, inside <a href={base + '/components/avatar'}>the avatar</a> if it is a person or an org</td>
            <td><strong>A mask cannot do this.</strong> It discards the artwork's own colours
            by design — that is what makes it themable. Multi-colour means an image</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">A per-tenant app tile</th>
            <td>the app launcher's inline <code>svg</code> slot</td>
            <td>Already the extension point: an inline <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> inherits
            <code>currentColor</code> exactly like the shipped glyphs</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">An initials avatar</th>
            <td><strong>no SVG at all</strong> — <a href={base + '/components/avatar'}><code>.bo-avatar</code></a> with two letters in it</td>
            <td>Text scales, translates, is selectable and needs no asset pipeline.
            Generating an SVG for it would be strictly worse</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      <strong>What this framework will not ship: an illustration set.</strong> Empty
      states and error screens are the places an ERP feels most broken, and the fix is
      almost always a clear sentence and the action that resolves it — not a drawing.
      A picture that has to be redrawn for every tenant's brand, translated for nothing,
      and downloaded on a warehouse tablet is cost without an answer. If you want one,
      it is an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>, and it is <code>alt=""</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

